d of its growth. It is a theory of reason that assigns to rational arguments the modest and yet important role of criticizing our often mistaken attempts to solve our problems...\" - Preface."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Introduction : on the sources of knowledge and of ignorance -- Science : conjectures and refutations -- The nature of philosophical problems and their roots in science -- Three views concerning human knowledge -- Towards a rational theory of tradition -- Back to the Presocratics -- A note on Berkeley as precursor of Mach and Einstein -- Kant's critique and cosmology -- On the status of science and of metaphysics -- Why are the calculi of logic and arithmetic applicable to reality? -- Truth, rationality, and the growth of scientific knowledge -- The demarcation between science and metaphysics -- Language and the body-mind problem -- A note on the body-mind problem -- Self-reference and meaning in ordinary language -- What is dialectic? -- Prediction and prophecy in the social sciences -- Public opinion and liberal principles -- Utopia and violence -- The history of our time : an optimist's view -- Humanism and reason."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"by Karl R. Popper."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references and indexes."}],"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Methodology"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Prediction (Logic)"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Knowledge, Theory of"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Science","Methodology"]},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Science"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Logic"}],"classification":{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"BD241 .P65x 1968"},"relatedItem":[{"@type":"series","titleInfo":{"title":"Harper torchbooks ","partNumber":"TB 1376"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990002199880203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0061313769"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^^83672449^"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"1028503"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/0000000460635171","#text":"Center for Hellenic Studies Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"BD241 .P65x 1968"},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no2014094889","#text":"Widener Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"BD241 .P65x 1968"},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","#text":"Kirkland House Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"BD241 .P65 1968"},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/0000000460377627","#text":"Ernst Mayr Library of the Museum of Comparative Zoology, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"BD241 .P65 1968"},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/000000046073724X","#text":"Cabot Science Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"BD241 .P6"},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/0000000123374118","#text":"Baker Library, Harvard Business School, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"BD241 .P65 1968"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":["Baker Business","Cabot Science","Ctr Hellen Studies","Kirkland","Museum Comp Zoology","Widener"]},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/990002199880203941","priorrecordids":{"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALEPH","#text":"000219988"}},"processingDate":"2026-10-16T04:11Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"MH"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"831201"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20020606"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"990002199880203941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.8 using\n\t\t\t\t\tMARC21slim_MODS3-8_XSL2-0.xsl (Revision 2.79 2025/03/28)"}},{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"title":"Conjectures and refutations ","subTitle":"the growth of scientific knowledge "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":["Popper, Karl R. (Karl Raimund),",{"@type":"date","#text":"1902-1994"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"enk"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"England"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"London "}},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York "}}],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Routledge"},"dateIssued":"1989","edition":"5th ed. (rev.)."},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xiii, 431 p. ; 24 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"\"The way in which knowledge progresses, and especially our scientific knowledge, is by unjustified (and unjustifiable) anticipations, by guesses, by tentative solutions to our problems, by conjectures. These conjectures are controlled by criticism: that is, by attempted refutations, which include severely critical tests. They may survive these tests; but they can never be positively justified: they can neither be established as certainly true nor even as 'probable' (in the sense of the probability calculus). Criticism of our conjectures is of decisive importance: by bringing out our mistakes it makes us understand the difficulties of the problems which we try to solve. This is how we become better acquainted with our problem, and able to propose more mature solutions: the very refutation of a theory - that is, of a tentative solution to our problem - is always a step forward that takes us nearer the truth. And this is how we can learn from our mistakes. As we learn from our mistakes our knowledge grows, even though we may never know - that is, know for certain. Since our knowledge can grow, there can be no reason here for despair of reason. And since we can never know for certain, the can be no authority here for any claim to authority, for conceit over our knowledge, or for smugness. The essays and lectures of which this book is composed apply this thesis to many topics, ranging from problems of the philosophy and history of the physical and social sciences to historical and political problems.\""},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"by Karl R. Popper."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references and indexes."}],"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Methodology"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Knowledge, Theory of"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Science","Methodology"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Prediction (Logic)"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":["Science","history"]}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"BD241 .P65 1989"},{"@edition":"20","@authority":"ddc","#text":"121"}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0415043182"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^^92226938^"},{"@type":"lccn","@invalid":"yes","#text":"^^^92220039^"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"27072584"}],"relatedItem":{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990055746110203941/catalog"}},"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/000000011940971X","#text":"Harvard Law School Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"BD241 .P65 1989"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":"Law School"},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.